# tls_native_client_auth.tcyr — 6.6.14 (CVE-64): a native server that asks for a client
# certificate AUTHENTICATES the client, over TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.2 alike, on every host target.
#
# THE DEFECTS (premise-checked on 6.6.13):
#   1. the TLS 1.2 server sent no CertificateRequest and never read the verify mode. A server ctx
#      accepts 1.2 by default, so ANY client that offered only TLS 1.2 completed the handshake
#      UNAUTHENTICATED against a server that required a certificate — and the 1.2 client failed
#      every server that asked for one (TLS_ERR_BAD_HANDSHAKE).
#   2. the TLS 1.3 server checked POSSESSION (the CertificateVerify against the presented leaf),
#      never a chain: a self-signed, expired or foreign-CA leaf was an identity, and a server with
#      no trust roots accepted every client.
#   3. tls_set_verify mapped every non-zero OpenSSL mode to TLS_VERIFY_PEER, so
#      SSL_VERIFY_FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT never reached native.
#   4. the native peer getters read the client-side slot, so a server could not see who connected.
#
# HOW IT RUNS EVERYWHERE, SINGLE-THREADED (threads run inline on cass and ach, and native TLS does
# not run over Winsock sockets at all): both ends are the REAL drivers — tls_native_connect, and
# lib/tls.cyr's tls_accept_alloc_in + tls_accept_complete with the consumer's hook calling
# tls_set_verify — over an in-memory transport (tls_native_set_transport) with a deterministic
# entropy source (tls_native_set_entropy) and a pinned clock. A row is REPLAYED to a fixed point:
# each round runs the client from scratch against everything the server wrote last round, then the
# server from scratch against everything the client just wrote; a read past the end of the log is
# EOF. Determinism (seeded entropy per role, RFC 6979 ECDSA, PureEdDSA) makes every replay of a
# side byte-identical on its common prefix, so each round moves the handshake one flight further,
# and the row ends at the first round in which neither side read past the other's log (that round
# replays itself exactly) or neither log grew. The client then sends "ping" and the server answers
# "pong", so a row that completes proves both ends hold the same keys.
#
# ROWS, each in TLS 1.3 and with the client pinned to TLS 1.2 (the server keeps 1.2..1.3): no
# certificate under PEER (accepted, no identity) and under FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT; P-256, Ed25519
# and P-384 leaves from the trusted CA; refusals — an untrusted self-signed leaf, a foreign CA's
# leaf, an expired leaf, a serverAuth-only leaf, the trusted leaf signed by another key, a server
# with no roots; possession — the CertificateVerify removed by a tampering transport, and (1.2) an
# unrequested CertificateVerify after an empty Certificate; and a server that does not ask.
# MUTATIONS measured RED (6.6.14): the 1.2 CertificateRequest dropped (37 assertions), the chain
# check dropped (40), tls_set_verify's FAIL bit dropped (6), the 1.2 client's CertificateRequest
# branch disabled (37), the getters reading SERVER_CERT (12), the clientAuth EKU check dropped
# (8), the 1.2 CertificateVerify verdict ignored (4), the 1.2 CertificateVerify not read (21), the
# 1.2 leftover-message check dropped (1), the 1.2 FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT check disabled (2). The
# 6.6.13 lib fails 45 of the 193.
#
# The fixtures (P-256 CA "mtls-ca", every leaf valid 2025-01-01 .. 2049-12-31 except the expired
# one, notAfter 2027-01-01) are read at a pinned clock of 2030-01-01, so no row is a time bomb.
include "lib/assert.cyr"
include "lib/alloc.cyr"
include "lib/string.cyr"
include "lib/io.cyr"
include "lib/tls.cyr"

enum MxHandle { MX_CLIENT = 1; MX_SERVER = 2; }
var MX_LOG_CAP = 262144;
var MX_ROUNDS_MAX = 10;

# The fixtures: DER certificates and their PKCS#8 keys (generated with openssl for this test).
var _mx_ca = 0;
var _mx_ca_n = 363;
